.TH DQL-EXAMPLES 1 "2026-07-17" "dql 0.1.0" "DelightQL Manual"
.SH NAME
dql\-examples \- worked, copy\-pasteable examples: the CLI flags in
practice, then the language itself
.SH DESCRIPTION
Every example on this page was executed against the binary it ships in
before it was written down. Most language examples use anonymous tables
(\fB_( ... )\fR) so they run with no database at all \(em paste them
straight into
.B dql query
and they work. Examples that need a database state their schema inline.
.SH INVOCATION \(em THE FLAGS IN PRACTICE
Three ways to supply the query text:
.EX
$ dql query --db app.db 'users(*), age > 30'      # inline argument
$ echo 'users(*) ~> count:(*)' | dql query --db app.db    # stdin
$ dql query --db app.db --file report.dql --sequential
.EE
With
.BR \-\-sequential ,
a multi\-statement file executes in order and the \fBfinal\fR
statement's result is returned; earlier statements run for their
effects (mounts, consults, writes).
.PP
See the SQL instead of running it \(em for any dialect:
.EX
$ dql query --to sql -f raw '_(x @ 1)'
$ dql query --dialect postgres --to sql -f raw '_(x @ 1)'
.EE
.PP
Stable identities for regression testing:
.B \-\-to hash
(data only, column names ignored),
.B \-\-to totalhash
(schema + data \(em a column rename changes it),
.B \-\-to fingerprint
(the full JSON record):
.EX
$ dql query --to hash -f raw '_(x @ 1)'
.EE
.PP
Output formats: \fB-f\fR \fBtable\fR (default), \fBbox\fR, \fBjson\fR,
\fBjsonl\fR, \fBcsv\fR, \fBtsv\fR, \fBlist\fR, \fBraw\fR.
.PP
Query JSON from a pipe as a relation \(em destructure with the
tree\-unify operator \fB~=\fR, then use the whole language on it:
.EX
$ echo '[{"name":"Ada","age":36},{"name":"Alan","age":41}]' \e
    | dql tools jstruct 'j(j), j ~= ~> {name, age} |> -(j)'
name  age
Ada   36
Alan  41
.EE
.PP
Every error carries an addressable badge; the badge is a door, not a
dead end:
.EX
$ dql explain parse/pony
$ dql explain semantic/arity
.EE
.SH QUERIES
.SS Anonymous tables \(em data with no database
.EX
_(x @ 1)                     # one row, one column
_(a, b @ 1, 2 ; 3, 4)        # header @ rows; ; separates rows
.EE
.SS Positional patterns \(em destructuring, not projection
A table access is a pattern that must cover \fBevery\fR column: bind a
position with a name, discard it with \fB_\fR, keep everything with
\fB*\fR. Assuming \fBusers(user_id, name, age)\fR:
.EX
users(_, name, _)            # just the names
users(uid, name, age), age > 30
.EE
.SS The join IS the shared variable
Two patterns binding the same bare name unify \(em no JOIN, no ON:
.EX
_(uid, name @ 1, "ada") as u, _(uid, total @ 1, 9.5) as o
uid  name  total
1    ada   9.5
.EE
Caution: \fBglob\fR access qualifies its names, so
\fBusers(*), orders(*)\fR shares no bare names and cross\-joins.
Join globs with an explicit condition
(\fBusers.id = orders.user_id\fR) or bind the key argumentatively.
.SS Pipelines: group, aggregate, order, limit
.EX
_(dept, n @ "eng", 1 ; "eng", 2 ; "ops", 5)
  |> %(dept ~> sum:(n) as total)
  |> #(total descending), # < 2
.EE
\fB|>\fR is the pipe; \fB%()\fR groups (left of \fB~>\fR = keys, right
= aggregates); \fB#()\fR orders; \fB# < 2\fR limits.
.SS Computed columns \(em and the no\-PEMDAS rule
.EX
_(n @ 3) |> +( (n * 2) as dbl )
.EE
DelightQL has \fBno operator precedence\fR: \fBa * b + c\fR does not
parse. Group every composition: \fB((a * b) + c)\fR.
.SS Rename, project, project\-out
.EX
_(a, b @ 1, 2) |> *( a as x )    # rename, everything else passes
_(a, b @ 1, 2) |> (b)            # keep only b
_(a, b @ 1, 2) |> -(a)           # drop a, keep the rest
.EE
.SS Aggregates, bare and filtered
.EX
_(n @ 1 ; 2) ~> count:(*)                # whole-relation reduction
_(n @ 1 ; 2 ; 3) ~> count:(n | n > 1)    # | filters the aggregate
.EE
.SS Case analysis
.EX
_(n @ 2 ; 5) |> +( _:( n > 3 -> "big"; _ -> "small") as size )
.EE
.SS Null is spelled with = (there is no `is null`)
\fB=\fR is the null\-safe equality (IS NOT DISTINCT FROM); \fB==\fR is
traditional SQL equality where NULL never matches:
.EX
_(x @ 1), x != null          # "x is not null"
.EE
.SS Recursion \(em a CTE that references its own name
.EX
_(n @ 1) : nums
nums(*), n < 100 |> (n + 1 as n) : nums
nums(*) ~> sum:(n)           # 5050
.EE
.SS Semi\-join and anti\-join
With \fBpeople(id, name, age)\fR and \fBgrownups(name, age)\fR:
.EX
people(_, name, _), +grownups(name)     # names WITH a grownups row
people(_, name, _), \e+grownups(name)    # names WITHOUT one
.EE
.SS Reshaping: melt (wide to long)
.EX
_(region, q1, q2 @ "north", 10, 20), _( quarter, amount
                 ----------------
                 "Q1", q1;
                 "Q2", q2 ) |> (region, quarter, amount)
region  quarter  amount
north   Q1       10
north   Q2       20
.EE
.SS Tree groups \(em a query shaped like the tree it returns
.EX
_(dept, n @ "eng", 1 ; "eng", 2 ; "ops", 5)
  ~> { dept, "items": ~> {n} }
[{"dept":"eng","items":[{"n":1},{"n":2}]},
 {"dept":"ops","items":[{"n":5}]}]
.EE
.SH RULES AND EFFECTS
Rule definitions live in files, not at the prompt. Given
\fBmylib.dql\fR:
.EX
adults(uid, name) :- people(uid, name, age), age >= 18
.EE
consult it, enlist it, call it:
.EX
$ dql query --db app.db --sequential '
consult!("mylib.dql", "mylib")
enlist!("mylib")
adults(_, name)'
.EE
A write is a relation too \(em evaluate it, get a receipt back:
.EX
_(msg @ "hello") |> insert!(log(*))(*)
success  operation  target
1        insert!    log
.EE
User \fBeffect rules\fR (rules whose evaluation writes) also live in
files and execute inside a run \(em never as a bare prompt invocation.
Given \fBjob.dql\fR:
.EX
route!(*) :- people(_, name, age), age >= 18 |> insert!(grownups(*))(*)
main!(*)  :- route!(*)
.EE
.EX
$ dql query --db app.db --sequential '
consult!("job.dql", "job")
run_namespace!(job)(*)'
.EE
.SH THE FIVE TRAPS
The mistakes every newcomer makes, in one place:
.IP 1. 3
\fBPatterns are not projections.\fR \fBusers(name, age)\fR on a
3\-column table is an arity error \(em cover every position
(\fBusers(_, name, age)\fR) or use \fB*\fR.
.IP 2. 3
\fBThe pipe is |>\fR, feeding relational operators
(\fB%() #() +()\fR). \fB~>\fR is the aggregate separator.
.IP 3. 3
\fBNo PEMDAS.\fR \fBa * b + c\fR does not parse; group every
composition explicitly.
.IP 4. 3
\fBNo `is null`.\fR Write \fBcol = null\fR / \fBcol != null\fR.
.IP 5. 3
\fB_( is one token.\fR \fB_ (id @ 1)\fR (with a space) does not
parse.
